tacts and here  you can see that each stimulation in  each contact was able to produce a  visual phosphine it's a different size  different shape different location for  every area that was stimulated and it  respected the horizontal Meridian as  we'd expect according to the calcarine  sulcus so this was  very encouraging with consistent  localization over time this is an  example of the shape and location of  percepts across sessions showing that  you know when we stimulate a certain  area across sessions they would see it  in the same or this particular subject  we see it in the same spot over a month  of testing so that consistency over time  though the lack of plasticity is  actually a good thing for developing a  visual cortical prosthesis  we also learned a lot about the  stimulation biology of the visual cortex  and this is something that came out you  know there's a lot of literature about  what it takes to activate neurons and  what we found was that the stimulation  parameters for fostering productions  shares two basic characteristics of the  strength duration curve and neuronal  excitation and again without getting  into too much detail I'll say that these  slides show that there's a real base  current or a minimum amplitude of  infinite pulse width frequency and that  is present so there's a sort of a  minimum current that must occur in order  to elicit a response or a phosphate  there's also again a minimum charge is  regarded to elicit a phosphate and the  extra charge increases linearly as the  pulse width or frequency increases and  those are illustrated here these are  very important concepts in terms of  determining how a chronic stimulation  system would have to be programmed and  adjust it perhaps the more important  slide is getting some insight into how  these phosphate perceptions are changed  by different stimulation parameters so  although you might think that it's total  current that affects how people perceive  a phosphate what we see here is that the  charge per trial is not what determines  the absolute intensity rather you can  see for example under a1 the dotted line  shows the perceived intensity of a  phosphine as the frequency is modulated  whereas the solid line shows the  perceived intensity as the amplitudes  modulator you can see that for the same  charge per trial an approximately  ten microcoulombs the perceived  intensity can be dramatically different  and we see that in almost every single  contact so it's not just charge that  we're interested in amplitude modulation  and frequency modulation can  differentially affect the patient's  perceptions with chronic visual  stimulation so this work was really  essential in providing us the motivation  to really charge forward with a  purpose-built visual prosthesis system  which is called Orion which leverages  the existing technology from Argos the  same idea of having an implanted array  wirelessly connected through an RF coil  with a video processing unit glasses  with a video camera  the advantage of visual cortical  prosthesis is it bypasses the optic  nerve goes directly to the visual cortex  we did some preclinical testing to prove  the safety of this implant in terms of  large animal studies to confirm that  there wasn't any adverse effect from the  implanted stimulation and then we of  course did the device the trial that I  just showed you with a chronic  stimulation with a non purpose-built  device this is were fortunately funded  by the brain initiative this shows  schematically what the implant looks  like and in the first panel you see the  glasses with an RF coil and a video  processing unit in the corner the two  brain schematics showed the roughly  triangular shaped array over the medial  occipital lobe with a cable and a skull  mounted array where we coupled two and  communicate with device this was based  on cadaveric large animal and  feasibility studies and at this point  using this device design we've implanted  six subjects across two sites and the  plan at this point is to follow these  six sites six subjects across the two  sites at UCLA and Baylor with the  neurosurgeon leading at Baylor being dr.  Danny oh sure with a primary outcome of  safety but secondary outcome of really  assessing the feasibility the ability to  produce phosphines long-term  functionality device and benefit in  terms of visual functioning functional  vision and quality of life this is an  example of what the implant looks like  it's a u-shaped incision there's a small  craniotomy seen in the middle panel  where the cable runs into the inner  hemispheric fissure where the array is  placed over the medial occipital lobe  you can see the device and the RF coil  to the left and that's the incision  there as with any technique we start a  little bit larger with our approach in  order to make sure feasibility  anticipate that this will get smaller  over time to date with the UCL a  subjects we have an average age of  implant of 51 female 3 males and the  causes of blindness are shown there  trauma pediatric blind as someone who  has a pediatric condition that evolves  into complete blindness and glaucoma and  that highlights really how this device  is potentially useful from multiple  diagnoses rather than Argos which is  currently only improved retinitis  pigmentosa implant duration the shortest  duration at this point is nine months  and we have someone who's implanted over  a year and we're very happy with the  results so far as I said safety is the  major issue and there has been one  serious adverse event which is a seizure  which has been resolved that's something  that was anticipated could be a problem  it's been in a single subject and we've  had four non serious adverse events as  well with hand twitching and a visual  aura as well as two events of headaches  and there's two patients who have had no  adverse events in three subjects with no  serious adverse events in the UCL a  cohort in terms of our thresholds this  is something that we need to understand  so we know how much power a device like  this needs to have thresholds are  determined based on being able to see a  phosphate 50% of the time you can see  the fossa  the average threshold is grinding around  two to two and a half million across  subjects and that has been consistent  over time and across contact as you can  in subject 156 out of the sixty contacts  are they able to elicit a phosphine 60  out of 60 in the second subject and 59  out of 59 and 59 59 in the third and  fourth subject showing very high  reliability of producing phosphenes so  we've done some preliminary studies in  light localization staring of screen  with a bright box where patients have to  point to the light and early testing has  been a very positive almost all of our  subjects are able to do that with high  reliability as well as direction of  motion testing more details will follow  as we analyze that data and presented in  subsequent presentations our research  focus in the first six months has really  been to establish the safety established  what our thresholds are and then create  video settings and establish simulation  safety setting so people can go home  with these devices we've cleared all of  our subjects at UCLA for home use so  they take at home and use it up to two  two hours a day to explore their home  and see how the device can be used  effectively we've also initiated  rehabilitation sessions so it's very  important to realize that these patients  aren't going to have normal vision  restored but they need to learn to adapt  to this new form of vision and it's not  to say that it's not useful but it's  something new in some ways you can  consider it like sensory substitution  and they need to learn how to use it and  identify the pathways to making it the  most effective therapy possible for them  one of the issues we've been focusing on  is this issue of spatial mapping you  know in the Argus 2 with a retinal  implant there was a one-to-one spatial  mapping meaning if you stimulate in a  pattern that looks like an age you would  anticipate that a patient would see in  the form of an H as we know the visual  cortex doesn't have that type of mapping  and so just because an array looks a  certain way stimulating a certain  pattern or an H as we see here in blue  dots on the array does not translate  into an H in the visual space because of  the organization of the visual cortex  this is an area of that we are studying  extensive  we're trying to understand how we should  do the spatial mapping in order to make  the device as useful as possible note  that I already said that the light  localization is very good direction  emotions have things already very good  so even without having a deep  understanding of the optimal way of  doing spatial map and we still have very  promising results but we need to develop  an algorithm for efficient and accurate  spatial mapping in order to make this  useful in one area we've looked at is  you know is from the imaging literature  we have a lot of information about how  polar angles are mapped in the visual  cortices as well as eccentricity is maps  individual cortices and here you can see  an Orion array with the white dots  overlaid in that area and one of the  questions is you know how reliably can  we predict where the phosphene will be  based on these population level maps are  these population level maps the best way  to map out the visual space and get the  device up and going or do we need  individualized maps as part of the  investigations that we're doing  currently to better refine the system  for home use I want to start closing out  my presentation with a couple examples  of real world use and patient  experiences that will help you really  understand what this is like for  patients I'll take some time to read  these these are feedback from the vision  rehab specialist it's the first one he  was able to find the cue ball