 what our  occlusion primary visual cortex  stimulation is feasible we've seen that  with our first patient we didn't have a  purpose-built device with the neuro pay  system and we're also seeing it with  early results from our feasibility study  here the simulation parameters are  predictable based on basic science  insights into the physiology of neuronal  excitation phosphenes are reproducible  to consistent over time we're now is  trying to trying to better understand  the modulation of these phosphenes  temporal factors so how quickly can you  stimulate to develop a constant  perception or if you want to develop a  flickering perception how what are the  temporal parameters we need to  understand a frame of reference and then  real-life applications some other issues  that I will just briefly talk about and  I've mentioned already these temporal  issues the flicker fusion experiments  are in our future that we need to do  spatial resolution issues you know how  do we need to make the array even more  precise or is this basically what we're  going to get how further work do we need  to do these are questions we need to  address I've talked about spatial  mapping the importance of rehabilitation  has become very clear over time  because if this is not a plug-and-play  solution it's a people need to learn  just like with any real ability  rehabilitative device how to use it  optimally and then this idea of sensory  substitution finally and there are  ethical issues with restoring some kind  of sensation or ability to patients as  well as the people who enroll these  trials and expectations they have and  there's something we need to consider  very carefully so I hope I've been able  to give you some insight into our work  in the visual cortical prosthesis thank  you for your time thank you for that  exciting talk on this cutting edge  technology dr. brought in as a reminder  our speaker will follow many questions  about his presentation by email next we  have dr. 