 with no  problems on the table and note that  these are patients who have no like  reception or bear-like perception I  wouldn't have been able to do this  before he's able to tell the cue ball  from the blue ten and also from the  balls with the stripe versus the cue  ball he could find the rack balls at the  other end of the table too here's  another example we found that looking  forward is where the Orion shines better  for the user as they can detect upcoming  objects he's able to see cars parked on  the side street openings in the sidewalk  up into driveways etc again so you know  the device is not the vision as we know  it but as a visual aid can be extremely  could be extremely useful especially if  integrated with other kinds of visual  aids the next one when working  throughout her apartment what sorry when  ya working through our apartment  she's able to tell where I was located  and when standing in front of a  ten-foot-wide light wall without digital  clutter she's also able to correctly  determine whether I was traveling from  left to right or right to left along  this well seven out of ten times again  pretty amazing considering these people  are have no light perception for the  most part finally he's able to order  patterns from small checkers big  checkers of white cloth there's a  half-inch difference and checker size in  the pattern so I think you know these  are very hard to quantify in clinical  trials were very focused on  quantification but the quantification  that we do in the clinic setting with  formal testing is not necessarily  representative real life and we think  these real life examples are really  where there is significant value and I  think what we're finding is the subjects  are finding success with Orion for  everyday tasks I will say that the the  amount of success is variable and not  every subject is able to use it the same  way it has to some extent to do with  expectation management so um I'll start  with you know what our what our  occlusion primary visual cortex  stimulation is feasible we've seen that  with our first patient we didn't have a  purpose-built device with the neuro pay  system and we're also seeing it with  early results from our feasibility study  here the simulation parameters are  predictable based on basic science  insights into the physiology of neuronal  excitation phosphenes are reproducible  to consistent over time we're now is  trying to trying to better understand  the modulation of these phosphenes  temporal factors so how quickly can you  stimulate to develop a constant  perception or if you want to develop a  flickering perception how what are the  temporal parameters we need to  understand a frame of reference and then  real-life applications some other issues  that I will just briefly talk about and  I've mentioned already these temporal  issues the flicker fusion experiments  are in our future that we need to do  spatial resolution issues you know how  do we need to make the array even more  precise or is this basically what we're  going to get how further work do we need  to do these are questions we need to  address I've talked about spatial  mapping the importance of rehabilitation  has become very clear over time  because if this is not a plug-and-play  solution it's a people need to learn  just like with any real ability  rehabilitative device how to use it  optimally and then this idea of sensory  substitution finally and there are  ethical issues with restoring some kind  of sensation or ability to patients as  well as the people who enroll these  trials and expectations they have and  there's something we need to consider  very carefully so I hope I've been able  to give you some insight into our work  in the visual cortical prosthesis thank  you for your time thank you for that  exciting talk on this cutting edge  technology dr. brought in as a reminder  our speaker will follow many questions  about his presentation by email next we  have dr. Michael Bushnell and his  colleague dr. John McNaughton he will be  giving another innovative presentation  on rave our analysis and visualization  of intracranial encephalography hope to  see you there
 

